David Damerell wrote:
> begin  quoting  Klaus Kassner  <Klaus.Kassner@physik.uni-magdeburg.de>:

> I think the lesson we learn from Marvin's record is that, while you _can_
> lose from bad luck, almost all the deaths you feel are such are actually
> lack of skill.

I don't agree.  I also had three or four ascensions in a row (and 
ascended all twelve classes of NH 3.1.3 within a span of 30 games).
But most of my early deaths are not due to stupidity (I would say :-) ) 
but rather to not finding good equipment in time and being forced to 
dive deeper to avoid starvation. Then situations accumulate where 
monsters start taking away half of my Hp in one round of attacks, 
because I still don't have the means to improve my AC or to kill them 
fast.  Finally I die; in most of the cases not by stupidity, but because 
there was just no way to equip myself, due to a "too empty" dungeon (too 
empty in objects, not monsters).  On the other hand, none of my 
characters getting down to AC -15 (-16 for slashem) ever died (well, two 
did but were saved by an amulet of life saving).

> For example, even hill orcs have a MONSTR of 4; you'd have to be XL 5 for
> that to happen. Why so high an XL?

XL 5 is very low.  And traps with poisoned spikes don't depend on your XL.

> Again, the tell-tale boulder lying in a room is a dead giveaway.

It happened to me while I was XL 1 and I was hit after entering the room 
through the door.  No way to see the boulder before.

>>I would be all in favor of eliminating these inevitable deaths, but I
>>don't see any way. 

> I don't believe it's possible to have all of;
> a) roguelike-style interest through randomly generated situations.
> b) a challenging game.
> c) no excessively challenging situations that kill you unfairly.

> Angband largely eliminates c) through the player's unlimited ability to
> retreat without penalty. That largely eliminates b) for cautious play,
> save for very deep in the dungeon.

In nethack you can also retreat often.  The game is still good enough at 
creating lots of situations that are 'er interesting (i.e. challenging).

> Crawl is a fascinating game because of its high challenge level; but it
> indisputably does sometimes kill you without mercy. No-one will ever win
> 12 in 13 playing Crawl.

Never played it.

> NetHack, I think, has a reasonable balance early on, but could do with a
> stiffer challenge later - even at the cost of some small proportion of
> unavoidable deaths. 

I agree mostly.  Except that I think that the balance is not fully 
reasonable for all classes.  A physically weak class should have a 
strategy available.  Tourists and healers do, but I don't think that 
priests have one.  On the other hand, slashem is much worse in terms of 
balance.  Very difficult at the beginning for some classes, much easier 
than nethack in later game, simply because there is so much stuff around 
that you can find everything you need with high probability.

> You still sometimes get enormously sticky situations -
> Double Rodney and Demogorgon on a no-tele level springs to mind - but very
> infrequently; I'd rather see a higher rate of occurrence of moderately
> sticky situations, which the game is bad at generating for high-level
> characters.

I agree but they should not increase the probability to die beyond one 
percent for a player, say, of Marvin's level :-).
